From 16972ae12aa46c3af5487b34d6f033aac514ee7d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: tomaszstaniak <68373859+tomaszstaniak@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 00:29:33 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] stdc: __close_stdio() restores flags only on the caller's own
 handles

A stdcio base caches the Input()/Output() handles of the process that
opened it. A library's root base can keep such a base until the library
is expunged: png.library has "rellib posixc", so it holds a posixc base
and, through it, a stdcio base from its first opener. Expunge runs from
the low-memory handlers, in whichever task hit a failed allocation or
ran Avail FLUSH, long after the first opener closed those handles.
__close_stdio() then writes fh_Flags through a stale pointer into freed
or reused memory. A write watchpoint caught it setting FHF_NOBUF in a
DosList node's dol_Next, after which DOS no longer found later volumes.

Restore the mode bits only when the cached handle is still the calling
process's pr_CIS or pr_COS. A plain task has neither and restores nothing.

Verified: on nightly 20261031 with this stdcio.library installed, a
failed malloc, Avail FLUSH and a C++ program whose std::bad_alloc came
from a failed exec allocation each left the DOS list intact in 3 of 3
runs; stock gave 3 of 3, 3 of 3 and 7 of 7 damaged. setvbuf() modes are
still restored on exit, and output buffered before a stdcio close in a
program without C startup still reaches a redirected file.

Tested on: x86_64-pc ABIv1, GCC 10.5, QEMU TCG. Not tested: real
hardware, other architectures, a full build of the branch.
---
 compiler/crt/stdc/__stdio.c | 19 +++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 17 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/compiler/crt/stdc/__stdio.c b/compiler/crt/stdc/__stdio.c
index ea42a2ebb0..725f6b27d3 100644
--- a/compiler/crt/stdc/__stdio.c
+++ b/compiler/crt/stdc/__stdio.c
@@ -67,6 +67,8 @@ static int __close_stdio(struct StdCIOIntBase *StdCIOBase)
 {
     struct FileHandle *_fh;
     FILE *stream;
+    struct Process *me;
+    BPTR cis = BNULL, cos = BNULL;
 
     D(bug("[%s] %s: StdCIOBase = 0x%p, DOSBase = 0x%p\n", STDCNAME, __func__, StdCIOBase, DOSBase));
 
@@ -80,14 +82,27 @@ static int __close_stdio(struct StdCIOIntBase *StdCIOBase)
        started the C runtime itself, so the Shell's Close() dropped the
        program's buffered tail and its script stopped (2026-09-16). */
 #define STDCIO_FHF_MODEBITS (0x00000004 | 0x00000008)   /* FHF_LINEBUF | FHF_NOBUF */
-    if (StdCIOBase->intstdout.fh)
+    /* The base can outlive the handles it cached. A library's root base
+       is closed when the library is expunged, by whichever task ran the
+       low-memory handlers, long after the process it was opened in closed
+       them; the write below then lands in freed memory (seen as a DosList
+       node's dol_Next gaining FHF_NOBUF). Only a handle that is still this
+       process's own can be restored. */
+    me = (struct Process *)FindTask(NULL);
+    if (me->pr_Task.tc_Node.ln_Type == NT_PROCESS)
+    {
+        cis = me->pr_CIS;
+        cos = me->pr_COS;
+    }
+
+    if (StdCIOBase->intstdout.fh && StdCIOBase->intstdout.fh == cos)
     {
         _fh = BADDR(StdCIOBase->intstdout.fh);
         _fh->fh_Flags = (_fh->fh_Flags & ~STDCIO_FHF_MODEBITS)
                       | (StdCIOBase->intstdout.fhFlags & STDCIO_FHF_MODEBITS);
     }
 
-    if (StdCIOBase->intstdin.fh)
+    if (StdCIOBase->intstdin.fh && StdCIOBase->intstdin.fh == cis)
     {
         _fh = BADDR(StdCIOBase->intstdin.fh);
         _fh->fh_Flags = (_fh->fh_Flags & ~STDCIO_FHF_MODEBITS)
-- 
